.Dd October 16, 2026
.Dt NG_POLICY 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ng_policy
.Nd netgraph identity-aware policy enforcement node type
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In netgraph/ng_policy/ng_policy.h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
node type performs stateless, identity-aware policy enforcement on
Ethernet frames.
It registers
.Em endpoints ,
each a pair of hooks, evaluates every frame crossing one of those hooks
against a precompiled, priority-ordered rule set, and either forwards the
frame to the endpoint's other hook or drops it.
.Pp
The node compiles nothing itself.
Rules, endpoint registrations and an IPv4-to-endpoint address map arrive
over its own netgraph control socket already in binary form, and are
swapped into place atomically as a single immutable
.Em generation .
Translating tenant intent, security groups, labels or any other
higher-level concept into endpoint identifiers and IPv4 rules is entirely
a userland concern.
.Pp
The node knows endpoint identifiers, IPv4 addresses, protocols, ports,
ICMP type and code, actions and generation numbers.
It knows nothing about tenants, VPCs, subnets, jails, virtual machines,
labels, applications, environments or clusters, and it makes no
forwarding decision of its own: given a frame and the endpoint its
ingress hook fixes, it decides
.Dv NG_POLICY_ALLOW
or
.Dv NG_POLICY_DROP
and, if allowed, hands the frame to the one pre-wired egress hook for
that endpoint pair.
It never consults a routing table, never selects among candidate egress
hooks, and never learns topology from traffic.
.Ss Fail-closed by default
A freshly constructed node is at generation 0 with a default action of
.Dv NG_POLICY_DROP
and mode
.Dv NG_POLICY_MODE_ENFORCE .
It blocks all traffic until a policy generation is committed, or until
the mode is set to
.Dv NG_POLICY_MODE_DISABLED .
This is deliberate and is almost always the first thing to surprise
someone experimenting by hand.
.Sh HOOKS
Hooks are created in pairs, one pair per endpoint: a workload side and a
fabric side.
A frame allowed on one is forwarded to the other.
.Pp
The names
.Va hook
and
.Va peer_hook
are given to
.Dv NGM_POLICY_ENDPOINT_ADD
and are arbitrary.
The conventional spellings are
.Ql ep-%u
and
.Ql fab-%u
.Pq Dv NG_POLICY_HOOK_EP_FMT , NG_POLICY_HOOK_FAB_FMT ,
but hook names carry no authority whatsoever: the numeric endpoint
identifier assigned by
.Dv NGM_POLICY_ENDPOINT_ADD
is the only identity the node recognises.
.Pp
Binding is lazy and symmetric.
A hook may be connected before or after its endpoint is registered;
both orders reach the same state.
A frame arriving on a hook that belongs to no registered endpoint is
dropped and counted in
.Va unbound_hook_packets .
.Pp
.Va peer_hook
may be the empty string, in which case the endpoint has no egress: all
of its traffic is dropped and counted in
.Va no_peer_packets .
.Sh ENDPOINTS
An endpoint identifier is a 32-bit value.
Valid identifiers for registration run from 1 to
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_ID_MAX
.Pq 0xfffffeff ;
the following are reserved and may appear in rules but never in a
registration:
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv NG_POLICY_EP_LOCAL_HOST"
.It Dv NG_POLICY_EP_ANY
.Pq 0x00000000
Matches any endpoint when used in a rule.
.It Dv NG_POLICY_EP_LOCAL_HOST
.Pq 0xfffffffd
The host itself.
.It Dv NG_POLICY_EP_EXTERNAL
.Pq 0xffffffff
The endpoint an IPv4 address that the address map does not resolve is
attributed to.
.El
.Pp
Everything from
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_RESERVED_MIN
.Pq 0xffffff00
upward is reserved by the ABI.
.Pp
Endpoint flags are split by lifetime.
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_ENABLED
and
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_TRUSTED
are persistent configuration;
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_F_REPLACE
is accepted on a request and never stored; and
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_S_CONNECTED
and
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_S_PEER_CONNECTED
are set by the kernel in list responses only.
Note that
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_ENABLED
is reserved and not enforced in this version:
.Dv NGM_POLICY_ENDPOINT_DEL
is the only way to disable an endpoint.
.Sh OPERATING MODES
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv NG_POLICY_MODE_PERMISSIVE"
.It Dv NG_POLICY_MODE_DISABLED
.Pq 0
Frames are forwarded without any rule lookup.
.It Dv NG_POLICY_MODE_PERMISSIVE
.Pq 1
Rules are evaluated and the outcome counted, but a frame that policy
would have dropped is forwarded anyway and counted in
.Va permissive_violations .
.It Dv NG_POLICY_MODE_ENFORCE
.Pq 2
Rules are evaluated and enforced.
.El
.Pp
The mode is set with
.Dv NGM_POLICY_SET_MODE
or through
.Dv NGM_POLICY_SET_CONFIG ,
and is node configuration rather than part of a policy generation.
.Sh PACKET CLASSIFICATION
Frames are classified before rule matching, and each class that is not
ordinary IPv4 has its own configurable action in
.Vt struct ng_policy_config :
.Va arp_action ,
.Va frag_action ,
.Va unsupported_action
and
.Va malformed_action .
.Pp
ARP is handled outside L4 policy entirely.
Non-initial IPv4 fragments never reach rule matching, because the
ports they would be matched on are not present in them.
EtherTypes other than IPv4 and ARP, VLAN-tagged frames included, take
.Va unsupported_action .
A frame too short or otherwise inconsistent for its claimed headers
takes
.Va malformed_action .
.Pp
An IPv4 protocol number that is not TCP, UDP or ICMP is
.Em not
classed unsupported: it is matched by rule, against rules whose
.Va protocol
is that number or
.Dv NG_POLICY_PROTO_ANY ,
with the port fields treated as the full range.
.Sh RULES AND MATCHING
A rule is
.Vt struct ng_policy_rule ,
32 bytes with no implicit padding.
Matching is first-match wins over a set ordered by
.Va priority
ascending, ties broken by the order the rules were added within the
transaction.
.Pp
A rule matches when the source and destination endpoints, the protocol,
and the source and destination port ranges all match.
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_ANY
in either endpoint field is a wildcard, as is
.Dv NG_POLICY_PROTO_ANY
in
.Va protocol
and a port range of
.Bq Dv NG_POLICY_PORT_ANY_MIN , NG_POLICY_PORT_ANY_MAX .
.Pp
ICMP type and code are matched through the dedicated
.Va icmp_type
and
.Va icmp_code
fields, each enabled by its own flag
.Pq Dv NG_POLICY_RULE_F_ICMP_TYPE , NG_POLICY_RULE_F_ICMP_CODE ,
never by overloading the port ranges.
.Pp
Only
.Dv NG_POLICY_ALLOW
and
.Dv NG_POLICY_DROP
are accepted as actions.
Values 3 through 7 are reserved for future REJECT, LOG, RATE_LIMIT,
REDIRECT and MIRROR semantics and are rejected by this version rather
than silently treated as something else.
.Pp
If no rule matches, the generation's default action applies, set with
.Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_SET_DEFAULT .
.Sh THE ADDRESS MAP
Each generation carries a map from IPv4 address to endpoint identifier,
used to attribute the far side of a frame.
Addresses are in network byte order, and 0.0.0.0 is invalid.
An address the map does not resolve is attributed to
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_EXTERNAL ,
so an unmapped peer is a policy subject rather than a hole.
.Pp
With
.Dv NG_POLICY_CFG_F_STRICT_ENDPOINTS
set in the node configuration, rules are additionally validated against
the registered endpoint table at commit time.
.Sh POLICY GENERATIONS
Policy is replaced wholesale, never edited in place.
A generation is built in a transaction and committed with a single
pointer swap, so a frame observes generation
.Em N
in full or
.Em N+1
in full and never a mixture.
.Bl -tag -width ".Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_SET_DEFAULT"
.It Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_BEGIN
Opens a transaction and returns its
.Va tx_id ,
the generation number it will produce, the currently active generation
and the deadline in seconds.
A
.Va requested_generation
of 0 means the active generation plus one; a
.Va timeout
of 0 means
.Va net.graph.policy.tx_timeout .
.It Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_ADD_RULE
Adds a batch of rules.
.It Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_ADD_ADDR
Adds a batch of address-map entries.
.It Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_SET_DEFAULT
Sets the generation's default action.
.It Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_COMMIT
Commits.
.Va base_generation
must equal the active generation, or the commit is refused with
.Er ESTALE
and nothing changes \(em optimistic concurrency, so that two writers
cannot silently lose one another's work.
.Dv NG_POLICY_TXC_F_FORCE
overrides that check.
The response reports the new generation, the memory the snapshot
occupies, the rule and address counts, and the build, validate, commit
and reclaim costs in microseconds.
.It Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_ABORT
Discards a transaction and its memory.
.El
.Pp
Rules and addresses are batched because a hundred thousand single-rule
messages is not a design.
A transaction left open past its deadline is reaped automatically and
counted in
.Va tx_timeouts .
.Sh CONTROL MESSAGES
All messages use the
.Dv NGM_POLICY_COOKIE
type cookie.
The versioned request structures carry a
.Va version
field in which 0 means
.Dq current ABI ,
so that
.Xr ngctl 8
stays usable for hand debugging without typing a version every time; any
other value must equal
.Dv NG_POLICY_ABI_VERSION
or the message is rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
The ASCII names below are what
.Xr ngctl 8
accepts.
.Bl -tag -width ".Cm getendpointstats"
.It Cm getconfig , setconfig
Read and write
.Vt struct ng_policy_config :
mode and the four per-class actions.
The default action is not here; it belongs to the generation.
.It Cm setmode
Set the operating mode alone.
.It Cm endpointadd , endpointdel
Register and unregister an endpoint.
.It Cm endpointlist
Enumerate endpoints, windowed.
.It Cm txbegin , txaddrule , txaddaddr , txsetdefault , txcommit , txabort
Build and commit a generation, as above.
.It Cm getstats , resetstats
Read and reset node-wide counters.
.It Cm getendpointstats
Per-endpoint counters.
.It Cm getrulestats
Per-rule match counters, windowed.
.El
.Ss Response paging
.Dv NGM_POLICY_ENDPOINT_LIST
and
.Dv NGM_POLICY_GET_RULE_STATS
return windowed responses: the caller passes
.Va start_index
and
.Va max_entries ,
and the reply carries
.Va total ,
.Va returned
and
.Va next_index
to continue from.
.Pp
A single response never exceeds
.Dv NG_POLICY_BATCH_MAX
.Pq 512
entries, and each handler additionally clamps its entry count so that
the whole reply fits
.Dv NG_POLICY_RESP_BYTE_BUDGET
.Pq 20480 bytes ,
which is the default
.Va net.graph.recvspace
bound on a single netgraph control datagram.
A caller asking for more than fits simply gets a smaller page and keeps
paging.
The module cannot observe an individual caller's socket options, so a
caller that has raised
.Va net.graph.recvspace
is still served pages sized off the default and merely pages more often.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width ".Va net.graph.policy.debug_force_copy"
.It Va net.graph.policy.max_endpoints
Maximum endpoints per node.
Default 65536.
.It Va net.graph.policy.max_rules
Maximum rules per generation.
Default 262144.
.It Va net.graph.policy.max_addrs
Maximum address-map entries per generation.
Default 262144.
.It Va net.graph.policy.max_tx
Maximum concurrent open transactions per node.
Default 16.
.It Va net.graph.policy.max_tx_bytes
Maximum memory all open transactions on a node may hold.
Default 67108864.
.It Va net.graph.policy.tx_timeout
Default transaction deadline in seconds.
Default 60.
.It Va net.graph.policy.debug_force_copy
Force the mbuf-copy path in the parser, for testing.
Default 0.
.It Va net.graph.policy.nodes
Number of
.Nm
nodes currently constructed.
Read-only.
.It Va net.graph.policy.commits , net.graph.policy.failed_commits
Module-wide commit counters.
Read-only.
.El
.Sh STATISTICS
.Dv NGM_POLICY_GET_STATS
returns
.Vt struct ng_policy_stats :
the active generation, packet and byte totals, the allowed, dropped,
permissive-violation, malformed, unsupported, fragmented, ARP,
unbound-hook and no-peer counts, rule and default match counts, address
lookups and hits, commit, failed-commit, abort and timeout counts, the
current mode, the endpoint, rule, address and transaction counts, and
the memory held by the policy snapshot, open transactions, the endpoint
table and the address map.
.Pp
The node-wide invariant is
.Pp
.Dl packets_total == allowed_packets + dropped_packets + no_peer_packets
.Pp
and it is checked by the module's own test suite under concurrent
policy replacement.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a node, read its configuration, and tear it down.
Note the fail-closed starting state in the reply:
.Pq Va mode=2 , enforce
with a default action of drop.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas ngctl -f - <<'EOF'
mkpeer policy ctl ctl0
name .:ctl pol0
msg pol0: getconfig
shutdown pol0:
EOF
.Ed
.Pp
Register two endpoints and commit one generation permitting
.Li 1001 -> 2001
on TCP port 443.
Two rules, not one:
.Nm
is stateless, so allowing the forward direction does not permit the
replies.
.Pp
Each
.Cm txaddrule
adds a batch and they accumulate, so the two rules are sent as two
messages here purely for legibility.
Note that
.Xr ngctl 8
has no line-continuation syntax: a message must be one line, however
long.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas ngctl -f - <<'EOF'
mkpeer policy ctl ctl0
name .:ctl pol0
msg pol0: endpointadd { endpoint_id=1001 hook="ep-1001" peer_hook="fab-1001" }
msg pol0: endpointadd { endpoint_id=2001 hook="ep-2001" peer_hook="fab-2001" }
msg pol0: txbegin { }
msg pol0: txaddrule { tx_id=1 nrules=1 rules=[ { rule_id=1 src_endpoint=1001 dst_endpoint=2001 src_port_min=0 src_port_max=65535 dst_port_min=443 dst_port_max=443 priority=10 protocol=6 action=1 } ] }
msg pol0: txaddrule { tx_id=1 nrules=1 rules=[ { rule_id=2 src_endpoint=2001 dst_endpoint=1001 src_port_min=443 src_port_max=443 dst_port_min=0 dst_port_max=65535 priority=10 protocol=6 action=1 } ] }
msg pol0: txaddaddr { tx_id=1 naddrs=1 addrs=[ { ipv4=10.0.0.5 endpoint_id=1001 } ] }
msg pol0: txsetdefault { tx_id=1 default_action=2 }
msg pol0: txcommit { tx_id=1 base_generation=0 }
msg pol0: getstats
shutdown pol0:
EOF
.Ed
.Pp
The commit reports what it built:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Rec'd response "txcommit" (11) from "[374]:":
Args:	{ generation=1 memory=472 build_usec=1 nrules=2 naddrs=1 }
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr ngctl 8
aborts the whole script at the first message that fails, so a
.Cm txcommit
refused with
.Er ESTALE
stops the script rather than leaving a half-applied policy.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Control messages fail with:
.Bl -tag -width ".Er ETIMEDOUT"
.It Er EINVAL
A malformed or inconsistent request: an unknown ABI version, a reserved
endpoint identifier where a real one was required, an action outside
.Dv NG_POLICY_ALLOW
and
.Dv NG_POLICY_DROP ,
a port list on a protocol that has no ports, an unset flag bit, or a
count that disagrees with the array that follows it.
.It Er ENOENT
A named endpoint, transaction or rule does not exist.
.It Er EEXIST
An endpoint with that identifier is already registered, and
.Dv NG_POLICY_EP_F_REPLACE
was not set.
.It Er EBUSY
A hook is already bound, or a transaction is in a state that forbids the
operation.
.It Er ENOSPC
A configured limit would be exceeded: see the
.Va max_*
sysctls above.
.It Er ENOMEM
Allocation failed.
.It Er ESTALE
.Dv NGM_POLICY_TX_COMMIT
with a
.Va base_generation
that is not the active generation.
Re-observe the active generation and recompute; never retry blindly.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr netgraph 4 ,
.Xr ng_bridge 4 ,
.Xr ng_ether 4 ,
.Xr ng_socket 4 ,
.Xr ngctl 8
.Pp
The module's own documentation set, under
.Pa docs/
in its source distribution, covers the architecture, the complete
control API, every packet outcome, the policy and endpoint models,
concurrency, statistics, security, measured performance and worked
.Xr ngctl 8
sessions.
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
node type first appeared in August 2026.
It is maintained out of the
.Fx
source tree.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An zombocoder Aq Mt zombocoder@gmail.com
.Sh CAVEATS
This version is stateless.
There is no connection table, so a rule permitting traffic in one
direction says nothing about the replies, and both directions of an
intended conversation must be emitted as separate rules.
.Pp
Only IPv4 is classified for L4 policy.
The address map carries 4-byte addresses, and IPv6 is not supported.
.Pp
.Xr tun 4
and
.Xr ng_iface 4
interfaces cannot participate.
.Nm
classifies traffic by parsing Ethernet frames, and neither device
carries Ethernet framing, so
.Xr ng_ether 4
attaches no netgraph node to them and there is no hook to wire
.Nm
into.
